# Embedded Sensor-Processing Pipeline for Anomaly Detection

Below is an initial implementation of an embedded system pipeline that processes accelerometer data to detect anomalies. This implementation uses a simple statistical approach for anomaly detection, which can be run on resource-constrained embedded devices.

## System Architecture

1. **Data Acquisition**: Read accelerometer data (x, y, z axes)
2. **Preprocessing**: Filter and normalize the data
3. **Feature Extraction**: Calculate statistical features
4. **Anomaly Detection**: Compare features against learned thresholds
5. **Action**: Trigger alerts or record anomalies

## Implementation (C++ for Embedded Systems)

```cpp
#include <cmath>
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>
#include <cstdint>

// Configuration parameters
constexpr uint16_t WINDOW_SIZE = 50;  // Number of samples in sliding window
constexpr float ANOMALY_THRESHOLD = 3.0f;  // Standard deviations from mean
constexpr float MOVING_AVG_ALPHA = 0.1f;  // For exponential moving average

// Data structures
struct AccelData {
    float x, y, z;
    uint32_t timestamp;
};

struct Features {
    float mean_magnitude;
    float std_dev;
    float max_magnitude;
    float min_magnitude;
};

// Circular buffer for sliding window
class CircularBuffer {
private:
    std::vector<AccelData> buffer;
    size_t head = 0;
    size_t tail = 0;
    bool full = false;

public:
    CircularBuffer(size_t size) : buffer(size) {}

    void add(const AccelData& data) {
        buffer[head] = data;
        if (full) {
            tail = (tail + 1) % buffer.size();
        }
        head = (head + 1) % buffer.size();
        full = head == tail;
    }

    bool isFull() const { return full; }

    const std::vector<AccelData>& getData() const { return buffer; }
};

// Anomaly detection class
class AnomalyDetector {
private:
    CircularBuffer dataBuffer;
    Features baselineFeatures;
    float movingAvgMagnitude = 0.0f;

public:
    AnomalyDetector() : dataBuffer(WINDOW_SIZE) {
        // Initialize baseline features (would normally be learned)
        baselineFeatures = {
            .mean_magnitude = 1.0f,  // Example values - would be calibrated
            .std_dev = 0.1f,
            .max_magnitude = 1.5f,
            .min_magnitude = 0.5f
        };
    }

    void processSample(const AccelData& sample) {
        // Add new sample to buffer
        dataBuffer.add(sample);

        // Only process when buffer is full
        if (!dataBuffer.isFull()) return;

        // Calculate current features
        Features currentFeatures = calculateFeatures(dataBuffer.getData());

        // Update moving average of magnitude
        movingAvgMagnitude = MOVING_AVG_ALPHA * currentFeatures.mean_magnitude +
                           (1 - MOVING_AVG_ALPHA) * movingAvgMagnitude;

        // Detect anomalies
        bool isAnomaly = detectAnomaly(currentFeatures);

        if (isAnomaly) {
            // Handle anomaly - in embedded system this might trigger an interrupt
            handleAnomaly(sample, currentFeatures);
        }
    }

    Features calculateFeatures(const std::vector<AccelData>& data) {
        Features features;
        float sum = 0.0f;
        float sum_sq = 0.0f;
        float max_val = -INFINITY;
        float min_val = INFINITY;

        for (const auto& sample : data) {
            // Calculate vector magnitude
            float magnitude = sqrt(sample.x*sample.x + sample.y*sample.y + sample.z*sample.z);

            sum += magnitude;
            sum_sq += magnitude * magnitude;
            max_val = std::max(max_val, magnitude);
            min_val = std::min(min_val, magnitude);
        }

        size_t count = data.size();
        features.mean_magnitude = sum / count;
        features.std_dev = sqrt((sum_sq / count) - (features.mean_magnitude * features.mean_magnitude));
        features.max_magnitude = max_val;
        features.min_magnitude = min_val;

        return features;
    }

    bool detectAnomaly(const Features& current) {
        // Simple statistical anomaly detection
        float z_score = (current.mean_magnitude - baselineFeatures.mean_magnitude) /
                       (baselineFeatures.std_dev + 1e-6f);  // Avoid division by zero

        // Check if magnitude is outside threshold standard deviations
        if (fabs(z_score) > ANOMALY_THRESHOLD) {
            return true;
        }

        // Check for sudden spikes or drops
        if (current.max_magnitude > baselineFeatures.max_magnitude * 1.5f ||
            current.min_magnitude < baselineFeatures.min_magnitude * 0.5f) {
            return true;
        }

        return false;
    }

    void handleAnomaly(const AccelData& sample, const Features& features) {
        // In embedded system, this might trigger an interrupt or log to flash
        // For this example, we'll just print
        printf("ANOMALY DETECTED at %u ms!\n", sample.timestamp);
        printf("  Magnitude: %.2f (baseline: %.2f ± %.2f)\n",
               features.mean_magnitude, baselineFeatures.mean_magnitude, baselineFeatures.std_dev);
        printf("  Max: %.2f, Min: %.2f\n", features.max_magnitude, features.min_magnitude);
    }

    // Method to update baseline features (would be called during calibration)
    void updateBaseline(const Features& newBaseline) {
        baselineFeatures = newBaseline;
    }
};

// Example usage
int main() {
    AnomalyDetector detector;

    // Simulate incoming accelerometer data
    for (uint32_t i = 0; i < 1000; ++i) {
        // Normal data
        AccelData sample = {
            .x = 0.1f * sin(i * 0.1f),
            .y = 0.1f * cos(i * 0.1f),
            .z = 1.0f,  // Gravity
            .timestamp = i
        };

        // Occasionally add an anomaly
        if (i % 200 == 0) {
            sample.x *= 5.0f;  // Sudden spike
        }

        detector.processSample(sample);
    }

    return 0;
}
```

## Key Components Explained

1. **Circular Buffer**: Efficiently stores the sliding window of accelerometer data without dynamic memory allocation.

2. **Feature Extraction**:
   - Calculates vector magnitude (sqrt(x² + y² + z²))
   - Computes mean, standard deviation, max, and min of magnitudes

3. **Anomaly Detection**:
   - Uses z-score to detect deviations from baseline
   - Checks for sudden spikes/drops in magnitude
   - Implements exponential moving average to smooth the signal

4. **Embedded Considerations**:
   - Minimal memory usage (fixed-size buffers)
   - Low computational complexity (O(n) per window)
